      "claim": "When the object distance do decreases from ∞ toward f (do > f), the image distance di = (f·do)/(do‑f) increases from f to +∞, i.e., the image moves farther from the mirror.",
      "evidence": "Algebraic rearrangement of the mirror equation; as do → f⁺ the denominator (do‑f) → 0⁺, so di → +∞.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The magnitude of the linear magnification is |m| = di/do = f/(do‑f); as do decreases toward f, |m| grows from 0 to ∞, so the image gets taller (more magnified).",
      "evidence": "Substituting di from step 2 into m = –di/do and simplifying gives |m| = f/(do‑f). The denominator shrinks to zero as do → f⁺, making |m| → ∞.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore, moving the object from far away toward the focal point makes the image move away from the mirror and become taller.",
      "evidence": "Combining the conclusions of steps 2 (image distance increases) and 3 (magnification increases).",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    }
